WEBVTT

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.202
Here are two readings from the tide board

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:03.282
at a harbour boat ramp:

00:00:04.103 --> 00:00:05.984
high tide, seven and a half metres, at

00:00:06.044 --> 00:00:06.965
time zero,

00:00:07.546 --> 00:00:09.387
and low tide, one and a half metres,

00:00:09.607 --> 00:00:10.388
six hours later.

00:00:11.309 --> 00:00:13.234
The water keeps rising and falling in the

00:00:13.295 --> 00:00:14.939
same pattern, about twice a day.

00:00:16.083 --> 00:00:18.007
A quantity that repeats like that is called

00:00:18.188 --> 00:00:18.789
periodic,

00:00:19.311 --> 00:00:21.455
and a transformed cos curve can describe it.

00:00:22.281 --> 00:00:24.051
Let's build that curve one feature at a

00:00:24.092 --> 00:00:24.292
time.

00:00:25.796 --> 00:00:26.540
Start with the middle.

00:00:27.125 --> 00:00:29.367
The water swings as far above its middle

00:00:29.409 --> 00:00:31.851
level as below it, so the midline is

00:00:31.952 --> 00:00:33.754
halfway between high and low:

00:00:34.376 --> 00:00:36.378
seven point five plus one point five,

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.042
over two, is four point five.

00:00:39.884 --> 00:00:42.248
The amplitude is the distance from the midline

00:00:42.469 --> 00:00:43.410
up to high tide:

00:00:43.972 --> 00:00:46.576
seven point five minus one point five, over

00:00:46.637 --> 00:00:47.998
two, is three.

00:00:48.741 --> 00:00:50.584
So the curve runs from four point five

00:00:50.666 --> 00:00:53.271
minus three, up to four point five plus

00:00:53.312 --> 00:00:53.532
three.

00:00:54.282 --> 00:00:54.947
Now the period.

00:00:55.651 --> 00:00:58.335
We assume the tide follows a smooth cos-shaped

00:00:58.395 --> 00:00:58.695
wave,

00:00:59.296 --> 00:01:02.020
and that these are consecutive tides, so high

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:03.802
to low is only half a cycle,

00:01:04.363 --> 00:01:06.386
and a full cycle, high back to high,

00:01:06.726 --> 00:01:07.748
takes twelve hours.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:11.441
For one cycle, the inside, a times t,

00:01:11.784 --> 00:01:12.747
has to grow by two pi.

00:01:13.895 --> 00:01:16.567
So a is two pi divided by twelve,

00:01:16.769 --> 00:01:17.793
which is pi over six.

00:01:18.558 --> 00:01:20.305
That's the period rule, read backwards:

00:01:21.049 --> 00:01:23.038
a equals two pi over the period.

00:01:23.949 --> 00:01:25.155
Which curve should we use?

00:01:25.859 --> 00:01:27.362
At time zero the water is at its

00:01:27.423 --> 00:01:29.808
highest, and the cos curve starts at its

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:30.049
peak.

00:01:30.672 --> 00:01:32.055
So no slide is needed:

00:01:33.079 --> 00:01:35.786
h equals three cos of pi t over

00:01:35.868 --> 00:01:37.672
six, plus four point five.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:39.550
Check the other measurement.

00:01:40.296 --> 00:01:43.037
At t equals six, the inside is pi,

00:01:43.718 --> 00:01:46.339
cos pi is minus one, and h is

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.300
minus three plus four point five:

00:01:48.941 --> 00:01:50.301
one point five, low tide.

00:01:51.342 --> 00:01:53.963
A sine model works too, with plus pi

00:01:54.004 --> 00:01:56.524
over two inside, which slides it left by

00:01:56.585 --> 00:01:59.126
three hours, but cos is simpler here.

00:01:59.843 --> 00:02:00.689
Now use the model.

00:02:01.152 --> 00:02:03.682
What's the water level four hours after high

00:02:03.722 --> 00:02:03.963
tide?

00:02:04.565 --> 00:02:06.992
The inside is four pi over six, which

00:02:07.052 --> 00:02:08.215
is two pi over three.

00:02:08.837 --> 00:02:10.720
Cos of two pi over three is exactly

00:02:10.901 --> 00:02:11.843
minus a half,

00:02:12.364 --> 00:02:15.189
so h is minus one point five plus

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:16.171
four point five:

00:02:16.672 --> 00:02:17.273
three metres.

00:02:18.055 --> 00:02:19.816
On the graph, the water is below the

00:02:19.857 --> 00:02:21.337
midline and still falling,

00:02:21.859 --> 00:02:23.719
as it should be, two hours before low

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:24.000
tide.

00:02:24.547 --> 00:02:25.208
Harder question:

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:28.033
when is the water level five point two

00:02:28.115 --> 00:02:30.138
five metres, in the first twelve hours?

00:02:31.001 --> 00:02:33.509
There's one cycle, and the level is between

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:36.097
low and high, so expect two times.

00:02:36.981 --> 00:02:39.446
Three cos of pi t over six, plus

00:02:39.488 --> 00:02:42.354
four point five, equals five point two five,

00:02:43.016 --> 00:02:45.462
so cos of pi t over six is

00:02:45.563 --> 00:02:45.963
a quarter.

00:02:47.025 --> 00:02:49.667
A quarter isn't an exact value, so use

00:02:49.828 --> 00:02:52.169
inverse cos on a calculator, in radians:

00:02:52.711 --> 00:02:55.293
pi t over six is about one point

00:02:55.374 --> 00:02:56.174
three one eight,

00:02:56.755 --> 00:02:59.197
and t is about two point five two

00:02:59.258 --> 00:02:59.597
hours.

00:03:00.459 --> 00:03:02.963
By symmetry, the other angle is two pi

00:03:03.125 --> 00:03:03.745
minus that,

00:03:04.287 --> 00:03:06.691
so t is about nine point four eight.

00:03:07.714 --> 00:03:09.576
So a boat that needs the level at

00:03:09.657 --> 00:03:12.061
least five and a quarter metres can come

00:03:12.082 --> 00:03:13.464
in for about the first two and a

00:03:13.505 --> 00:03:14.085
half hours,

00:03:14.607 --> 00:03:16.349
and again from about nine and a half

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:19.235
hours until twelve, in this first cycle.

00:03:19.958 --> 00:03:21.765
The model repeats that every twelve hours.

00:03:22.362 --> 00:03:23.429
Remember the Ferris wheel seat?

00:03:24.012 --> 00:03:25.154
Here's one with numbers:

00:03:25.694 --> 00:03:28.719
radius ten metres, its centre twelve metres above

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:31.404
the ground, turning once every sixty seconds.

00:03:32.266 --> 00:03:34.233
Follow one cabin, starting at the bottom.

00:03:35.037 --> 00:03:37.252
As the wheel turns, its height traces a

00:03:37.474 --> 00:03:37.756
wave.

00:03:38.478 --> 00:03:39.319
Midline twelve,

00:03:39.859 --> 00:03:42.320
amplitude ten, and period sixty,

00:03:42.821 --> 00:03:45.922
so a is two pi over sixty, pi

00:03:45.982 --> 00:03:46.443
over thirty.

00:03:47.244 --> 00:03:49.106
It starts at the lowest point, so we

00:03:49.127 --> 00:03:51.309
need the cos curve turned upside down:

00:03:52.212 --> 00:03:55.236
h equals twelve minus ten cos of pi

00:03:55.257 --> 00:03:56.197
t over thirty.

00:03:56.899 --> 00:03:57.139
Check:

00:03:57.723 --> 00:03:58.385
at thirty seconds,

00:03:58.929 --> 00:03:59.833
h is twenty two,

00:04:00.377 --> 00:04:00.617
the top.

00:04:01.315 --> 00:04:03.741
When is the cabin seventeen metres up, in

00:04:03.762 --> 00:04:04.524
the first turn?

00:04:05.467 --> 00:04:08.132
Twelve minus ten cos equals seventeen,

00:04:08.634 --> 00:04:11.278
so cos of pi t over thirty is

00:04:11.419 --> 00:04:12.360
minus a half.

00:04:13.144 --> 00:04:13.988
That's an exact value.

00:04:14.713 --> 00:04:17.014
Cos is minus a half at two pi

00:04:17.055 --> 00:04:19.436
over three and four pi over three, so

00:04:19.517 --> 00:04:20.717
t is twenty seconds,

00:04:21.238 --> 00:04:22.178
or forty seconds.

00:04:22.919 --> 00:04:24.783
On the wheel, those are the two moments

00:04:24.824 --> 00:04:26.787
the cabin passes seventeen metres,

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:28.691
once on the way up, and once on

00:04:28.712 --> 00:04:29.112
the way down.

00:04:29.721 --> 00:04:32.832
Here's a checklist for a sinusoidal model, a

00:04:32.872 --> 00:04:36.104
periodic quantity shaped like a smooth sine wave.

00:04:37.047 --> 00:04:37.288
Middle,

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:38.211
half,

00:04:38.753 --> 00:04:39.134
period,

00:04:39.635 --> 00:04:39.976
start.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.727
The midline is the middle of the max

00:04:42.847 --> 00:04:43.249
and the min.

00:04:43.873 --> 00:04:45.871
The amplitude is half the gap between them.

00:04:46.757 --> 00:04:48.510
a is two pi over the period.

00:04:49.175 --> 00:04:50.675
And the start picks the curve:

00:04:51.237 --> 00:04:52.516
cos if it starts at a max,

00:04:53.058 --> 00:04:54.578
minus cos if it starts at a min,

00:04:54.959 --> 00:04:56.599
and sine if it starts in the middle,

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:57.280
going up.

00:05:00.071 --> 00:05:00.554
Your turn.

00:05:01.019 --> 00:05:03.245
In a coastal town, the longest day has

00:05:03.366 --> 00:05:05.633
fourteen and a half hours of daylight, and

00:05:05.654 --> 00:05:08.000
the shortest day, six months later, has nine

00:05:08.041 --> 00:05:08.442
and a half.

00:05:09.005 --> 00:05:12.380
Assume a sinusoidal pattern, with these as consecutive

00:05:12.441 --> 00:05:12.922
extremes.

00:05:13.566 --> 00:05:15.509
Let t be the number of months after

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:18.114
the longest day, and model the daylight hours

00:05:18.235 --> 00:05:21.200
as D equals k cos a t, plus

00:05:21.241 --> 00:05:21.441
c.

00:05:22.165 --> 00:05:25.218
Find k, a and c, then find D

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:26.604
when t equals two.

00:05:27.313 --> 00:05:28.041
Pause here to answer.

00:05:38.681 --> 00:05:41.108
The midline c is the middle of fourteen

00:05:41.148 --> 00:05:42.532
and a half and nine and a half:

00:05:42.933 --> 00:05:43.214
twelve.

00:05:43.856 --> 00:05:46.384
The amplitude k is half the gap: two

00:05:46.404 --> 00:05:46.866
point five.

00:05:47.788 --> 00:05:49.812
Six months is half a cycle, so the

00:05:49.832 --> 00:05:52.579
period is twelve months and a is pi

00:05:52.599 --> 00:05:53.040
over six.

00:05:53.841 --> 00:05:55.845
At t equals two, cos of pi over

00:05:55.905 --> 00:05:58.530
three is a half, so D is twelve

00:05:58.611 --> 00:06:00.875
plus one point two five: thirteen point two

00:06:00.935 --> 00:06:01.436
five hours.

00:06:02.301 --> 00:06:04.493
An amplitude of five uses the whole gap,

00:06:04.856 --> 00:06:05.278
not half.

00:06:06.828 --> 00:06:07.272
Last one.

00:06:08.198 --> 00:06:11.101
A buoy's height, in metres, is h equals

00:06:11.382 --> 00:06:13.964
one point five sine of pi t over

00:06:14.105 --> 00:06:16.627
eight, plus three, where t is in seconds.

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:19.815
Find the first two times after t equals

00:06:19.856 --> 00:06:22.563
zero when h is three point seven five.

00:06:23.228 --> 00:06:24.120
Pause here to answer.

00:06:34.867 --> 00:06:37.688
One point five sine of pi t over

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:39.610
eight is point seven five,

00:06:40.151 --> 00:06:42.272
so sine of pi t over eight is

00:06:42.313 --> 00:06:42.752
a half.

00:06:43.595 --> 00:06:45.860
Sine is a half at pi over six

00:06:46.182 --> 00:06:47.605
and five pi over six.

00:06:48.428 --> 00:06:50.269
Multiply by eight over pi:

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.051
t is four thirds of a second,

00:06:53.592 --> 00:06:55.113
and twenty thirds of a second.

00:06:55.795 --> 00:06:58.363
Pi over six is the angle inside, not

00:06:58.384 --> 00:06:58.764
the time.

00:07:00.628 --> 00:07:03.573
So a sinusoidal model needs a midline, an

00:07:03.694 --> 00:07:06.039
amplitude, a period and a starting point.

00:07:06.801 --> 00:07:08.424
The water level only ranges from one and

00:07:08.445 --> 00:07:09.968
a half to seven and a half metres,

00:07:10.009 --> 00:07:12.213
so an ordinary axis shows it well.

00:07:13.197 --> 00:07:13.437
Next,

00:07:13.959 --> 00:07:16.465
quantities that range over many powers of ten.

00:07:17.509 --> 00:07:20.294
Here's a phone's storage: a text message, a

00:07:20.375 --> 00:07:23.080
photo, a film and the whole phone, on

00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:24.443
one ordinary ruler.

00:07:24.967 --> 00:07:26.017
Can you even see the text?

00:07:29.188 --> 00:07:30.071
Here are the key notes.

00:07:30.594 --> 00:07:32.362
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:32.383 --> 00:07:32.864
to copy them.
